esigned  in hundreds of years ago so that is kind  of the what attracts people to them and  besides they're also kind of a similar  thing to parks in that they have a  public function of just being a place to  relax or cool down or escape from hectic  city life or whatever so obviously they  should be pretty cheap in easily  accessible  it does strike me though that these  gardens are kind of different than your  typical Park Inn that people really only  go to these gardens to look at them and  sure - it's a sort of relaxing thing but  it's not the same thing as when you go  to like Hyde Park to play frisbee or to  go cycling or running or whatever really  these gardens are typically pretty quiet  and really people just go here to take  pictures just enjoy the view or  something like that and I guess in that  sense it is different from just going to  a typical Park you're usually not gonna  have a standard picnic in here which is  interesting because hundreds of years  ago these were very often made for  families to have events in and  festivities or even hunt or whatever and  nowadays they're really almost like an  open-air museum in a sense not quite but  it's I feel like it's somewhere in  between that and an actual park because  if you do go to Japanese cities they'll  have western-style parks as well so this  really kind of feels a unique role in  that now here's a little bit of foliage  which is a little bit more experimental  I wanted to try and make one of these  hills which is completely filled by  shrubbery not entirely sure how well  it's gonna work out but I think the next  episode will be an important point to  judge that because in the next episode  I'm gonna try to make the path to go to  the top of this mountain for a good view  of the rest of this garden so yeah  thanks for watching guys and I'll see  you next time  [Music]  you
 

